rallel edge gives a different path. A path reaching one target continues to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the others, so a path through a target to another is listed after the shorter one. The source","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"itself, when it is a target, gives the trivial path first.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The sequence holds the graph (a value), so iterating again gives the same paths. The first","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"next()"},{"text":" copies the rows the source reaches, O(n + m). A vertex that reaches no target is never","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"entered, but a partial path can still end where every way on is blocked by its own vertices,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"so the time between two paths is not bounded by a polynomial in n + m.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/traversal\/directedsimplepaths"]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Traversal\/documentation\/Traversal\/DirectedSimplePaths","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Traversal\/documentation\/Traversal"]]},"metadata":{"role":"symbol","modules":[{"name":"Traversal"}],"title":"DirectedSimplePaths","symbolKind":"struct","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"DirectedSimplePaths"}],"roleHeading":"Structure","remoteSource":{"fileName":"SimplePaths.swift","url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/Traversal\/SimplePaths.swift#L222"},"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"DirectedSimplePaths"}],"externalID":"s:9Traversal19DirectedSimplePathsV"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every simple path of a directed graph from a source to any of a set of targets, lazily, in the"},{"type":"text","text":" "}